# 13. Two Sum in BST

**a2z-348**

Find whether two nodes in a BST sum to `K`.

Example:

```text
        5
       / \
      3   6
     / \   \
    2   4   7
```

For:

```text
K = 9
```

we have:

```text
2 + 7
3 + 6
4 + 5
```

Answer:

```text
true
```

---

## Approach 1 — Inorder + HashSet

Traverse all values and use:

```text
needed = K - current
```

### Java

```java
public static boolean twoSumBST(TreeNode root, int k) {

    Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();

    return dfs(root, k, seen);
}

private static boolean dfs(
        TreeNode root,
        int k,
        Set<Integer> seen) {

    if (root == null)
        return false;

    if (seen.contains(k - root.val))
        return true;

    seen.add(root.val);

    return dfs(root.left, k, seen)
        || dfs(root.right, k, seen);
}
```

### C#

```csharp
public static bool TwoSumBST(TreeNode root, int k)
{
    HashSet<int> seen = new HashSet<int>();

    return Dfs(root, k, seen);
}

private static bool Dfs(
    TreeNode root,
    int k,
    HashSet<int> seen)
{
    if (root == null)
        return false;

    if (seen.Contains(k - root.Val))
        return true;

    seen.Add(root.Val);

    return Dfs(root.Left, k, seen)
        || Dfs(root.Right, k, seen);
}
```

Complexity:

```text
Time: O(N)
Space: O(N)
```

---

## Approach 2 — BST Iterators

This is the interesting interview follow-up.

Use:

```text
forward iterator
```

to get smallest → largest.

And:

```text
reverse iterator
```

to get largest → smallest.

Then use the classic two-pointer technique.

```text
left = smallest
right = largest

if left + right == K → true
if sum < K → move left
if sum > K → move right
```

### Complexity

```text
Time: O(N)
Space: O(H)
```

This is preferable when the interviewer asks:

> Can you use the BST property and reduce auxiliary memory?

