# 12. Merge Two BSTs

**a2z-347**

Given two BSTs, return their elements in sorted order.

---

## Approach 1 — Inorder arrays + merge

BST inorder is sorted.

```text
BST1 → sorted array
BST2 → sorted array
```

Then merge like merge-sort.

### Java

```java
public static List<Integer> mergeBSTs(
        TreeNode root1,
        TreeNode root2) {

    List<Integer> a = new ArrayList<>();
    List<Integer> b = new ArrayList<>();

    inorder(root1, a);
    inorder(root2, b);

    List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();

    int i = 0;
    int j = 0;

    while (i < a.size() && j < b.size()) {

        if (a.get(i) <= b.get(j))
            result.add(a.get(i++));
        else
            result.add(b.get(j++));
    }

    while (i < a.size())
        result.add(a.get(i++));

    while (j < b.size())
        result.add(b.get(j++));

    return result;
}

private static void inorder(
        TreeNode root,
        List<Integer> list) {

    if (root == null)
        return;

    inorder(root.left, list);
    list.add(root.val);
    inorder(root.right, list);
}
```

### C#

```csharp
public static List<int> MergeBSTs(
    TreeNode root1,
    TreeNode root2)
{
    List<int> a = new List<int>();
    List<int> b = new List<int>();

    Inorder(root1, a);
    Inorder(root2, b);

    List<int> result = new List<int>();

    int i = 0;
    int j = 0;

    while (i < a.Count && j < b.Count)
    {
        if (a[i] <= b[j])
            result.Add(a[i++]);
        else
            result.Add(b[j++]);
    }

    while (i < a.Count)
        result.Add(a[i++]);

    while (j < b.Count)
        result.Add(b[j++]);

    return result;
}

private static void Inorder(
    TreeNode root,
    List<int> list)
{
    if (root == null)
        return;

    Inorder(root.Left, list);
    list.Add(root.Val);
    Inorder(root.Right, list);
}
```

Complexity:

```text
Time: O(N + M)
Space: O(N + M)
```

---

## Approach 2 — Two BST iterators

Use two stacks.

Each stack exposes the next smallest element.

Then merge directly.

```text
Space: O(H1 + H2)
```

instead of storing all nodes.

This is the preferred follow-up.

