## The 5 BST patterns to actually memorize

Instead of memorizing 15 solutions independently, remember these:

### Pattern 1 — Search

```text
key < root → left
key > root → right
```

### Pattern 2 — Inorder

```text
BST inorder = sorted order
```

This solves/reduces:

```text
Validate BST
Kth smallest
Kth largest
Recover BST
Two Sum
Merge BSTs
```

### Pattern 3 — Candidate tracking

Used for:

```text
Floor
Ceil
Successor
Predecessor
```

### Pattern 4 — Range propagation

```text
validate(node, min, max)
```

Used for:

```text
Validate BST
Construct BST from preorder
```

### Pattern 5 — Postorder information propagation

```text
left info
right info
     ↓
current node
     ↓
return combined info
```

Used heavily in:

```text
Largest BST in Binary Tree
```

This last pattern is especially important for SDE2 interviews because it tests whether you can design a recursive function that returns **multiple pieces of information**, rather than solving every subtree independently. The O(N) postorder approach is also the expected complexity for the standard Largest BST problem. ([GeeksforGeeks][2])

